---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import ThoughtProcess from '../../components/ThoughtProcess.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const repo = 'https://github.com/omkar-k0/fuelcycle';
const demo = 'https://fuelcycle-seven.vercel.app';

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '3,297,846', label: 'French fuel price events in one year, every one checked by the gate', evidence: 'measured', source: 'silver_price_events, 1 Nov 2025 to 31 Oct 2026' },
  { value: '99.7%', label: 'of 586 planted errors caught, 2 false alarms in 118,795 clean events', evidence: 'measured', source: 'results/dq_eval.json, 14 real days' },
  { value: '92%', label: '"fill now" calls right, against 72% for always filling now', evidence: 'backtested', source: 'results/forecast_eval.json, 8 weeks rolling origin' },
  { value: '—', label: 'precision on the 7,050 real anomalies it flags: needs hand labels', evidence: 'pending', source: 'not labelled yet' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Data engineering + analysis' },
  { label: 'When', value: 'November 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo: adapter, pipeline, quality gate, evaluation, forecast, demo' },
  { label: 'Scale', value: '3.3M events · 10,150 stations · 12 months · 54 s rebuild on 3 GB RAM' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Done for France · Germany adapter next' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'The gate' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'Thought process' },
  { id: 'decisions', label: 'Decisions' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'refuses', label: 'What it refuses to claim' },
  { id: 'broke', label: 'What broke' },
  { id: 'open', label: 'Still open' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="fuelcycle"
  question="Can you trust a year of fuel prices, and prove the checks work?"
  description="A DuckDB and dbt pipeline over 3.3 million French fuel price events: a data-quality gate scored by planting known errors, daily price marts, and a next-day forecast that answers only when it is sure."
  crumb="Open source · 04"
  links={[
    { label: 'Open the demo page', href: demo, primary: true },
    { label: 'Source on GitHub', href: repo },
    { label: 'Gate evaluation', href: `${repo}/blob/main/results/dq_eval.json` },
  ]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  tldr={{
    problem: 'A year of station-level fuel prices is 3.3 million events. Most pipelines drop bad rows silently, so nobody can say how clean the result is.',
    result: 'Every event gets a verdict and a reason; the gate caught 99.7% of 586 planted errors with 2 false alarms. A next-day forecast answers on 3.4% of days, and its "fill now" call is right 92% of the time.',
    role: 'Solo: source adapter, bronze/silver/gold pipeline, quality gate, fault injection, forecast and demo.',
  }}
  reproduce={{
    repo,
    commands: `git clone ${repo} && cd fuelcycle
python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && make setup
make data        # French archives -> canonical daily files
make all         # pipeline, gate evaluation, forecast backtest, page data
make test        # end-to-end tests on a synthetic sample (what CI runs)`,
    files: [
      { path: 'results/dq_eval.json', what: 'Planted errors per type: injected, caught, right reason, false alarms' },
      { path: 'results/dq_eval_v1_hourly_peers.json', what: 'The earlier, weaker gate (68.8% on outliers), kept for comparison' },
      { path: 'results/forecast_eval.json', what: 'Backtest: error, band coverage, answer rate, fill-now and wait accuracy' },
    ],
    note: 'Price data from prix-carburants.gouv.fr under Licence Ouverte 2.0. It is downloaded, not stored in the repo.',
  }}
  stack={['DuckDB', 'dbt', 'Parquet', 'Python', 'LightGBM', 'Conformal prediction', 'GitHub Actions', 'Vercel']}
  prev={{ href: '/work/werkbank/', label: 'Werkbank' }}
  next={{ href: '/work/portfolio/', label: 'This website' }}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      France publishes every price change at every petrol station. I built a pipeline that loads a year of it,
      about 3.3 million changes, and checks every single one before anything is counted.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a price looks wrong, it gets a label saying why. Nothing is quietly thrown away. To prove the checks
      work, I planted 586 known errors in real data and counted how many were caught.
    </p>
    <p>
      On top of the clean data sits a forecast for "fill up today or wait?". It only answers on the days it is
      actually confident. On every other day it says "uncertain".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      How do you know your data is right, and how do you prove the checks themselves work?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Dropping bad rows in the load step</strong> makes the numbers look clean, but nobody can say how
      many rows went missing, or why.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Writing data tests and calling it done</strong> proves the tests run, not that they catch anything.
      A test that never fires looks the same as a test that is broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A forecast that always answers</strong> gives advice on days when tomorrow's price is a coin flip.
      For fuel prices that is most days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gate">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>The gate</h2>
    <p>Every price event ends up in exactly one of three states, each with a reason code.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Rules</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Quarantined</td><td>Unreadable, zero or negative price, outside 0.80–3.50 €, unknown station, timestamp outside the file's day, duplicate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flagged</td><td>A spike of more than 8% that reverts within an hour; more than max(30 ct, 6 MAD) from stations of the same type in the same département that day</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Accepted</td><td>Everything else</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The outlier rule abstains when fewer than 5 comparable stations reported that day. A dbt test checks every
      day that the number of events in equals the number of events out, so nothing can disappear.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="system">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Thought process</h2>
    <p>Left to right, the path one price change takes, from the government file to the forecast.</p>
    <ThoughtProcess
      steps={[
        { title: 'Adapt', points: ['French yearly XML', 'Old millième prices to euros', 'Paris time zone attached'], takeaway: 'One canonical format; a second country is one adapter.' },
        { title: 'Freeze', points: ['One Parquet file per day', 'Checksums in a manifest', 'Non-price files refused'], takeaway: 'Raw data kept as it came.' },
        { title: 'Judge', points: ['Six hard rules, two soft', 'A reason code on every flag', 'In = out, tested daily'], takeaway: 'Nothing is dropped silently.', model: true },
        { title: 'Aggregate', points: ['Price in force at noon (ASOF join)', 'France, 95 départements, 135 cities', 'Who reprices first'], takeaway: 'Daily grain, because the market moves daily.' },
        { title: 'Forecast', points: ['Quantile models, 3 fuels', 'Band calibrated on last week', 'Answer only if the band is one-sided'], takeaway: '"Uncertain" is an answer.' },
      ]}
      note="The French feed is converted into the same daily layout as Germany's Tankerkönig feed, so the pipeline after the adapter does not care which country the data comes from."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>Decisions, and what they cost</h2>
    <div class="decisions">
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Flag and keep</strong>
          <p>Every event gets a verdict and a reason, and the in = out test can prove nothing vanished.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Drop bad rows on load</strong>
          <p>Cheaper, but nobody could say what was lost. Cost: a bigger table and one more test per day.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Plant errors, then score</strong>
          <p>586 labelled errors in copies of 14 real days, scored on caught and right reason.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Trust that tests pass</strong>
          <p>Passing tests say nothing about what they would catch.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>Daily grain</strong>
          <p>In November 2025, 86% of station-fuel-days had exactly one price change.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>An hourly grid per city</strong>
          <p>It spilled about 2 GB a month to disk on a 3 GB machine and added nothing. Hourly stays switchable for Germany.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="decision">
        <div><span class="label">Chosen</span><strong>French open data now</strong>
          <p>Every price change, openly licensed, downloadable today.</p></div>
        <div><span class="label">Instead of</span><strong>Waiting for German data</strong>
          <p>Tankerkönig's history now needs a verified account and sign-ups were paused. Cost: not the local market yet.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What came out</h2>
    <h3>The gate, scored on planted errors</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Planted error</th><th>Planted</th><th>Caught</th><th>Right reason</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Duplicate</td><td class="n">67</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Zero or negative price</td><td class="n">65</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price out of range</td><td class="n">81</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regional outlier</td><td class="n">80</td><td class="n">97.5%</td><td class="n">97.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spike that reverts</td><td class="n">79</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Timestamp outside the file's day</td><td class="n">70</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unknown station</td><td class="n">62</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unreadable</td><td class="n">82</td><td class="n">100%</td><td class="n">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>All</strong></td><td class="n"><strong>586</strong></td><td class="n"><strong>99.7%</strong></td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="caption">Precision 99.7%: 2 false alarms among 118,795 clean events. 264 events were already flagged on the untouched copy; those are real anomalies with unknown truth and are not scored.</p>

    <h3>Fill up today?</h3>
    <figure class="shot">
      <a href={demo} rel="noopener">
        <img src="/shots/fuelcycle-forecast.webp" width="804" height="313" loading="lazy"
          alt="Line chart of the national E10 price over eight weeks in September and October 2026. The actual price, the forecast and its shaded 80% band move closely together. Orange dots mark days the model said fill now, during price rises; teal dots mark days it said wait, during the fall at the end of October." />
      </a>
      <figcaption class="caption">France, E10, the last 8 weeks of the backtest. Orange dots: "fill now". Teal dots: "wait". Every other day it answered "uncertain".</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Backtest, 19,679 area-fuel-days</th><th>Model</th><th>Baseline</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Error on tomorrow's price</td><td class="n">0.709 ct</td><td class="n">0.712 ct (tomorrow = today)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80% band contains tomorrow's price</td><td class="n">80.8%</td><td class="n">76.4% before calibration</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Days it answers</td><td class="n">3.4%</td><td class="n">–</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Fill now" right</td><td class="n">92%</td><td class="n">72% always fill now</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Wait" right</td><td class="n">44%</td><td class="n">28% always wait</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The point forecast is no better than "tomorrow = today": day-to-day prices are close to a random walk. The
      value is in knowing when not to answer. When it does say "fill now", it beats the base rate by 20 points.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="refuses">
    <h2><span class="num">08</span>What it refuses to claim</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>That the gate finds unknown errors.</strong> The planted errors were written alongside the rules.
      The score shows the rules work as built and rarely raise false alarms, not that they would catch a kind of
      error nobody thought of.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>That "wait" is good advice.</strong> It beats its base rate but is still wrong more often than
      right, so it should not be shown to users. That call came from this test window and has to hold on the
      next months.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>That some stations lead prices.</strong> Leaders and followers come out in about equal numbers,
      which is what chance gives when neighbours all reprice overnight.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="broke">
    <h2><span class="num">09</span>What broke</h2>
    <h3>DuckDB silently dropped rows</h3>
    <p>
      The in = out test failed on planted rows with an unreadable price: DuckDB's <code>UNPIVOT</code> drops
      NULLs by default. Fixed with <code>INCLUDE NULLS</code>. Without that test, those rows would have vanished.
    </p>
    <h3>81% of all flags were motorway stations</h3>
    <p>
      The first full run flagged 20,418 events, and 16,522 of them came from the 477 motorway stations. They are
      expensive by design, not wrong. Comparing each station with stations of the same type cut the flags to
      5,064.
    </p>
    <h3>Small peer groups hid outliers</h3>
    <p>
      Comparing stations within the same hour often left one or two peers, so an outlier was compared with
      itself. Only 68.8% of planted outliers were caught. Comparing over the whole day, and abstaining under 5
      peers, raised it to 97.5%.
    </p>
    <h3>The demo page shipped empty</h3>
    <p>
      A <code>.gitignore</code> line meant for raw data also matched the page's data folder, so every chart was
      blank on the first deploy. Now each chart loads on its own and the page says so when data is missing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2><span class="num">10</span>Still open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hand-label a sample of the 7,050 real flags to measure precision on real anomalies.</li>
      <li>A shuffled-time baseline for the leader/follower roles.</li>
      <li>Confirm "fill now" and "never show wait" on months the forecast has not seen.</li>
      <li>The German adapter for Tankerkönig once account access works, with the hourly marts switched on.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
